{"id":"workloads-management","corpus":"k8s","title":"Managing Workloads","sourceUrl":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/management/","license":"Kubernetes Documentation © The Kubernetes Authors, licensed CC BY 4.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","blocks":[{"kind":"h1","text":"Managing Workloads"},{"kind":"p","text":"You've deployed your application and exposed it via a Service. Now what? Kubernetes provides a number of tools to help you manage your application deployment, including scaling and updating."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Organizing resource configurations"},{"kind":"p","text":"Many applications require multiple resources to be created, such as a Deployment along with a Service. Management of multiple resources can be simplified by grouping them together in the same file (separated by --- in YAML). For example:"},{"kind":"p","text":"Multiple resources can be created the same way as a single resource:"},{"kind":"p","text":"The resources will be created in the order they appear in the manifest. Therefore, it's best to specify the Service first, since that will ensure the scheduler can spread the pods associated with the Service as they are created by the controller(s), such as Deployment."},{"kind":"p","text":"kubectl apply also accepts multiple -f arguments:"},{"kind":"p","text":"It is a recommended practice to put resources related to the same microservice or application tier into the same file, and to group all of the files associated with your application in the same directory. If the tiers of your application bind to each other using DNS, you can deploy all of the components of your stack together."},{"kind":"p","text":"A URL can also be specified as a configuration source, which is handy for deploying directly from manifests in your source control system:"},{"kind":"p","text":"If you need to define more manifests, such as adding a ConfigMap, you can do that too."},{"kind":"h3","text":"External tools"},{"kind":"p","text":"This section lists only the most common tools used for managing workloads on Kubernetes. To see a larger list, view Application definition and image build in the CNCF Landscape."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Helm"},{"kind":"p","text":"Helm is a tool for managing packages of pre-configured Kubernetes resources. These packages are known as Helm charts."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Kustomize"},{"kind":"p","text":"Kustomize traverses a Kubernetes manifest to add, remove or update configuration options. It is available both as a standalone binary and as a native feature of kubectl."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Bulk operations in kubectl"},{"kind":"p","text":"Resource creation isn't the only operation that kubectl can perform in bulk. It can also extract resource names from configuration files in order to perform other operations, in particular to delete the same resources you created:"},{"kind":"p","text":"In the case of two resources, you can specify both resources on the command line using the resource/name syntax:"},{"kind":"p","text":"For larger numbers of resources, you'll find it easier to specify the selector (label query) specified using -l or --selector, to filter resources by their labels:"},{"kind":"h3","text":"Chaining and filtering"},{"kind":"p","text":"Because kubectl outputs resource names in the same syntax it accepts, you can chain operations using $() or xargs:"},{"kind":"p","text":"The output might be similar to:"},{"kind":"p","text":"With the above commands, first you create resources under docs/concepts/cluster-administration/nginx/ and print the resources created with -o name output format (print each resource as resource/name). Then you grep only the Service, and then print it with kubectl get."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Recursive operations on local files"},{"kind":"p","text":"If you happen to organize your resources across several subdirectories within a particular directory, you can recursively perform the operations on the subdirectories also, by specifying --recursive or -R alongside the --filename/-f argument."},{"kind":"p","text":"For instance, assume there is a directory project/k8s/development that holds all of the manifests needed for the development environment, organized by resource type:"},{"kind":"p","text":"By default, performing a bulk operation on project/k8s/development will stop at the first level of the directory, not processing any subdirectories. If you had tried to create the resources in this directory using the following command, we would have encountered an error:"},{"kind":"p","text":"Instead, specify the --recursive or -R command line argument along with the --filename/-f argument:"},{"kind":"p","text":"The --recursive argument works with any operation that accepts the --filename/-f argument such as: kubectl create, kubectl get, kubectl delete, kubectl describe, or even kubectl rollout."},{"kind":"p","text":"The --recursive argument also works when multiple -f arguments are provided:"},{"kind":"p","text":"If you're interested in learning more about kubectl, go ahead and read Command line tool (kubectl)."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Updating your application without an outage"},{"kind":"p","text":"At some point, you'll eventually need to update your deployed application, typically by specifying a new image or image tag. kubectl supports several update operations, each of which is applicable to different scenarios."},{"kind":"p","text":"You can run multiple copies of your app, and use a rollout to gradually shift the traffic to new healthy Pods. Eventually, all the running Pods would have the new software."}],"lemmas":{"managing":["managing","manage"],"managings":["managings","managing"],"manages":["manages","manage"],"managed":["managed","manage"],"workloads":["workload","workloads"],"you'ves":["you'ves","you've"],"deployed":["deployed","deploy"],"deployeds":["deployeds","deployed"],"deploys":["deploys","deploy"],"exposed":["exposed","expose"],"exposeds":["exposeds","exposed"],"exposes":["expose","exposes"]